{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head 2202eadf3cc85cbdfc31c89ed476321ed441b3e1 on builder/u1-upgrade-application-20261117, all 44 artifacts, for request dcdbfa1b under my promise 42f5fb95. The implementation is U1 (request ea7faed9, promise 07c54062). The reporting artifact is the U1 note.\n\nThe head adds two commits to 9adfe7a0, which I reviewed in 836909f8. Its base is main 83371009, which is still origin main, so the merge tree equals the head's tree, 3dd8afb4.\n\n## The fix for 836909f8\n\n- `upgrade-rules.ts` adds a changeable `int` reference, `upgradeAt`, to the installation item. The in-place form of `application-upgrade-open` sets N's `upgradeAt` to the act's own position. The act opens its fetch at that position with ordinal 0, which `outside.ts` already requires (`request.operation === \"\u003cseq\u003e:0\"`).\n- `inPlaceOpen` reads operation `\u003cupgradeAt\u003e:0` from the ledger and holds while `operationSettled` is false. `application-upgrade-proof` now refuses `application-upgrade-pending` when either `pendingSuccessor` or `inPlaceOpen` holds, so the block covers both forms. A confirmed or refused outcome settles the operation and releases N with no clearing step, so no slot can be left stale. `operationSettled` and `operationId` come from `dap-derive` unchanged. No derive path is in the diff.\n- An `int` reference keeps the item within its 12 value slots and retains no entry below the floor. The note explains why a `fact` reference cannot serve.\n- Red (e) files an in-place act and then refuses a second in-place act and a replace act with `application-upgrade-pending`. A refused outcome then releases N without changing its release. The test repeats the same steps with a confirmed outcome, which moves N to the new release and then admits the next act. It ends by comparing a replay snapshot with the live state. `e.txt` shows 7 passing before the change and 1 failing with it. Its SHA-256 begins `ed92220a`, as the note's table says.\n- A fetch